  "code": "1092",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"226\"><strong data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"154\">Electrolyte analysis in body fluids</strong> (such as pleural, peritoneal/ascitic, or cerebrospinal fluid) helps in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"228\" data-end=\"555\">\n<li data-start=\"228\" data-end=\"291\">\n<p data-start=\"230\" data-end=\"291\">Differentiating <strong data-start=\"246\" data-end=\"262\">transudative</strong> from <strong data-start=\"268\" data-end=\"281\">exudative</strong> effusions</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"292\" data-end=\"382\">\n<p data-start=\"294\" data-end=\"382\">Identifying <strong data-start=\"306\" data-end=\"320\">infectious</strong>, <strong data-start=\"322\" data-end=\"335\">malignant</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"340\" data-end=\"353\">traumatic</strong> causes of fluid accumulation</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"383\" data-end=\"457\">\n<p data-start=\"385\" data-end=\"457\">Assessing <strong data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"431\">acid-base and osmotic imbalances</strong> in localized compartments</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"458\" data-end=\"555\">\n<p data-start=\"460\" data-end=\"555\">Detecting <strong data-start=\"470\" data-end=\"487\">urine leakage</strong> when compared with serum electrolytes (especially in ascitic fluid)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"557\" data-end=\"680\">&nbsp;Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Electrolyte</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Indication When Elevated in Fluid</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sodium</strong></td>\n<td>May suggest contamination with <strong>hypertonic urine</strong> (e.g., urinary ascites), or dehydration-related transudate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Potassium</strong></td>\n<td>May indicate <strong>cellular breakdown</strong> (e.g., malignancy, hemolysis), or <strong>urinary leakage</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chloride</strong></td>\n<td>Can be elevated in <strong>transudative effusions</strong>, or <strong>saline contamination</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bicarbonate</strong></td>\n<td>Rarely high in fluid; may suggest <strong>alkaline contamination</strong> or processing error</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"557\" data-end=\"680\">&nbsp;</p>",
